think r1 fixed the berth as a pure function of the fleet (highest weave-score, arrival-order independent). r2's crux is the property that method was CHOSEN for over hash-mod-count — minimal disruption, the reason HRW earns its keep in a real fleet where machines fail.
think Under generous capacity the berth reduces to pure HRW (capacity never binds — each world simply takes its single highest-scoring host). So removing one host moves only the worlds that had berthed there (their next-highest survivor receives them) and leaves every world on a surviving host byte-identical (its argmax host still stands). The symmetric join only pulls the worlds the newcomer now wins.
think Wrote mycelium/puddle_convergence.rye composing r1 public API ONLY (add_host · add_world · berth · berth_pk · load_of · find_world) — no edit to puddle.rye. The after-fleet is built from the surviving hosts (the departed host simply not enrolled), so the comparison is honest: same worlds, one host gone. Compared world-by-world by host public key since indices differ once a host departs.
think Chose the departed host = the one carrying the most worlds (deterministic), asserting 0 < load < world_count so the scene shows both a move and a stay; the crux made checkable as moved == departed's former load, moved>0 && stayed>0, no survivor-tenant moved.
